        "heading": "Shuffle Feel \u2014 Not Straight Eighths",
        "body": "Every pair of eighth notes in this tab is played as a long-short shuffle: the downbeat note gets roughly two-thirds of the beat, the upbeat gets one-third. This is marked as 'shuffle' feel \u2014 do NOT play the pairs evenly. If your comping sounds mechanical or march-like, you've lost the swing; listen to Robert Johnson and aim to match the lazy, loping groove."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Dominant-7 Boogie Pattern \u2014 Root + 5th/6th",
        "body": "Each chord is comped using the open root string paired with alternating 5th (fret 2) and 6th (fret 4) on the next string \u2014 this is the foundational Delta blues 'boogie' figure. Keep your index finger planted on the 5th-interval note and use your pinky or ring finger to reach the 6th. The motion is minimal: anchor, stretch, anchor, stretch."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Muting the Unused Strings",
        "body": "On the E7 shape you're only sounding strings 5 and 6; on A7, strings 4 and 5; on B7, strings 3 and 4. Rest the fleshy underside of your fretting fingers lightly against the unused strings to kill any sympathetic buzz. A muddy low-end ring is the most common beginner trap here \u2014 if it rings, something you're not trying to hit is vibrating."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Turnaround Lick \u2014 Chromatic Walkdown",
        "body": "Bars 7-8 form the turnaround: a stepwise chromatic descent on the low E (G\u2192F#\u2192F\u2192E) paired with the A string falling from B to A. This resolves the 12-bar back to the top. Play each note deliberately and let the half-step motion speak clearly \u2014 do not rush the descent into a smear. The quarter-note pulse on the first two beats gives the lick its authority before the eighth-note pairs kick in."
      },
      {
        "heading": "12-Bar Form Awareness",
        "body": "This is a 12-bar blues in E: four bars of E7, two of A7, two of E7, one of B7, one of A7, then two bars of E7 (with the turnaround on bar 11-12). Know where you are at all times \u2014 getting lost on the form is the most common mistake at this level. Count the bars out loud while you play until the shape is automatic."
      }
    ],
